\subsection{How Stan Initialization Works}{

When you call \code{fit_ento_model()} with \code{init = "auto"}, this function is used
to create an init function. Stan calls this function once per chain to get
starting values for the MCMC sampler.

\strong{Why random initialization matters:}
\itemize{
\item Different chains start from different points in parameter space
\item This helps diagnose convergence: if chains converge to similar values
from different starting points, we have more confidence in the results
\item Avoids getting stuck in local modes
}

\strong{What happens if parameters are not initialized:}
\itemize{
\item Stan uses its default initialization: uniform random on (-2, 2) for
unconstrained parameters, then transforms to constrained space
\item This usually works but can be inefficient for complex models
}
